From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Rui Figueira <rfigueira@cloudflare.com>
Date: Fri, 27 Nov 2026 16:09:47 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Serialize none-carrying legacy colors in their modern form
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

WPT now expects the specified value of a legacy rgb(), hsl() or hwb()
color with a none component to serialize like its computed value, as
Chrome does:

- rgb(none none none) is color(srgb none none none), not rgb(0, 0, 0).
- hsl() and hwb() keep the function, and their second and third
  components are always percentages: hsl(120 30 none) is
  hsl(120 30% none), not hsl(120 30 none).
- A relative color's origin and a color-mix() item serialize the same
  way: rgb(from rgb(none none none) r g b) is
  rgb(from color(srgb none none none) r g b), not
  rgb(from rgb(0, 0, 0) r g b).

This undoes the parts of "Preserve none color components through parse,
mix and serialization" that matched the older WPT rows: the specified
legacy-with-zeros form, the legacy text stamped on nested origins and
mix items, and the C1_AS_PERCENT/C2_AS_PERCENT flags that kept the
author's number or percent form.

Upstream Stylo writes the percentages for hsl() and hwb() already, but
writes rgb(0, 0, 0) for an rgb() with none, so Gecko has that bug.

WPT: css/css-color/parsing/color-valid-{rgb,hsl,hwb,relative-color}.html,
color-computed-relative-color.html, color-computed-color-mix-function.html
and color-valid-color-mix-function.html.

beep-boop-🤖
